forefathers -- returns the forefathers of a digraph
***************************************************

  * Usage: 
        L = forefathers (D, v)
  * Inputs:
      * D, a *note digraph: Digraph,, 
      * v, a *note thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, v must be a vertex of D
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note set: (Macaulay2Doc)Set,, a set of all the forefathers of v
        in D

Description
===========

The forefathers of a vertex v in a digraph D are all the vertexSet u in D such
that v is reachable from u. Another way to more intuitively see what the
forefathers are is to see the forefathers of a vertex v can be found by first
taking the parents of v. Then if you find the parents of each of the parents of
v, and continue the process until the list stops growing, this will form all
the descendants of v.

+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : D = digraph({a,b,c,d,e},{{a,b},{b,c},{b,d},{e,b}});|
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : forefathers (D, d)                                 |
|                                                        |
|o2 = set {d, e, a, b}                                   |
|                                                        |
|o2 : Set                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The forefathers of a vertex in a digraph are more commonly known as the
ancestors. But ancestors is an entirely different function in Macaulay 2, so
forefathers is the convention we will use

See also
========

  * *note isReachable: isReachable, -- checks if a vertex u is reachable from a
    vertex v
  * *note parents: parents, -- returns the parents of a vertex on a digraph

Ways to use forefathers:
========================

  * "forefathers(Digraph,Thing)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note forefathers: forefathers, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

