isEulerian -- determines if a graph or digraph is Eulerian
**********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        E = isEulerian G
        E = isEulerian D
  * Inputs:
      * G, a *note graph: Graph,, 
      * D, a *note digraph: Digraph,, 
  * Outputs:
      * E, a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, whether G or D is
        Eulerian

Description
===========

A graph is Eulerian if it has a path in the graph that visits each vertex
exactly once.  A digraph is Eulerian if it has a directed path in the graph
that visits each vertex exactly once.  Such a path is called an Eulerian
circuit.  Unconnected graphs can be Eulerian, but all vertices of degree
greater than 0 of a graph (or all vertices of degree greater than 0 in the
underlying graph of a digraph) must belong to a single connected component.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : bridges = graph ({{0,1},{0,2},{0,3},{1,3},{2,3}}, EntryMode => "edges");|
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : E = isEulerian bridges                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = false                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : D = digraph(toList(1..4), {{2,3},{3,4},{4,2}});                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : E = isEulerian D                                                        |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = true                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note hasEulerianTrail: hasEulerianTrail, -- determines whether a graph or
    a digraph has an Eulerian trail

